## SQL review and resolved design issues

- Unsafe consumption time does not violate the retained consumed_ms constraint and roll back a one-use denial. The implemented path uses explicit 0 as an unknown-time consumed tombstone for null/out-of-range initial samples, followed by immediate denial. 0 is a nonnull fence, not evidence of a fresh time or Auth observation. Range-safe clock rollback records its sample and denies without lowering the owner high-water. The zero sentinel covers the initial consumption sample only; the unchanged real clock function is nonnull, but a later artificially injected NULL inside old observe_now can raise/roll back. Unexpected SQL exceptions remain transaction rollback/uncertainty, not guaranteed consumption.
- Exact negative retirement now matches the allocation cleanup ID/fullclaim/owner/attempt/units and retained shared accounting records. Only a terminal unmarked allocation can release its occupied slot; marked uncertain allocations retain it, all cumulative holds remain. Wrong tuples cannot retire unrelated allocations.
- Existing-capacity inspection/replay enforces current pool enabled/quarantine/limit checks without requiring another remaining-quota reservation. New acquisition checks free slot/units; existing acquisition may be exactly at the limit but must deny if protected totals exceed a lowered limit.
- The safe initial consumption sample now advances the existing owner high-water before eligibility. A subsequent rollback below that sample cannot hide above the older proof-registration clock. The initial sample itself must not fall below the retained prior value.
- Approval quota-policy identity and existing allocation units/route-pool identity are now checked before a different quota/shared-pool lock. A post-wait denial can therefore retire only against the same pool already locked, avoiding a second-pool lock inversion under inconsistent metadata.
- The new eligibility helper sees the retained originally unconsumed local composite after the persistent proof is burned; the actual stored proof remains consumed. Original verified/expiry fields never refresh. Current-state checks repeat after quota/pool waits.

## Next dependency

After this slice passes actual SQL and aggregate verification, implement isolated versioned capacity marking using a distinct fresh inspect proof and the same locked v2 consumption/current helpers before and after quota/pool/global-operation locks. Retain exact operation identity, expected current pool version, immutable allocation, approval quota-policy binding, one marker and already-marked-as-observation semantics. Add exact nonretrying protocol-2 transport and actual SQL cases. Preserve existing exact close/reconcile/finality and accounting. Host wiring and the coordinated quiesced no-fallback route/privilege cutover remain later work; marking alone cannot enable provider dispatch.

Root completion/evidence/handoff scripts were independently reviewed: baseline 1573 tests/75 files/407 SQL cases, three new test files, 689→696 tree blobs, 686 preserved existing blobs, and guarded handoff versions 24/34/24→25/35/25 are consistent. The evidence script requires exactly all four successful check keys and frozen source hashes. Prior archive 269427 bytes and protected clean-handoff sections are preserved by assertions. Root corrected wording to distinguish marked uncertain provider outcomes from unmarked reservations and initial unknown-time consumption from later SQL-exception rollback.

Original v5 remains complete. P4 approval/expiry remain null, grant registry empty; P3b accounts/staging closed with retained holds. No paid Lovable edit, real model/voice call, connected SQL/settings change, activation, publication, AWS or billing action occurred. All 21 native cases, plus cross-backend/deployed Auth/provider/device acceptance, remain unrun.

